[00:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'The biggest green hydrogen plant ever built is finished. Construction on the NEOM or the NEOM green hydrogen project in Saudi Arabia is now complete. And the 8.4 billion US dollar facility'

[00:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'has officially entered its commissioning phase. 8.4 billion dollars. Commercial production is targeted for next year, 2027. Let me put the scale of this thing in perspective because the numbers are'

[00:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'staggering. The plant sits at Oxagon on the Red Sea coast in northwestern Saudi Arabia and it will produce 600 tons of carbon-free hydrogen every single day.'

[00:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'That hydrogen gets converted into green ammonia, up to 1.2 million tons of it every year. And shipped to global markets from a purpose-built jetty.'

[00:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Powering all of this is roughly 4 gigawatts of dedicated renewable generation, a solar farm the literal size of Manhattan producing 2.2 gigawatts plus 257 wind turbines, 257'

[01:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'adding another 1.6 gigawatts all feeding 2.2 gigawatts of electrolyzers. The largest electrolyzer'

[01:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'installation on the planet. Once it\'s running, the project will save around 5 million tons of CO2 every year. Now, why ammonia? By cuz by the way,'

[01:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'right next to me, as in just across the river here, there is an ammonia plant. And there\'s like steam pouring out of that thing every day.'

[01:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'It\'s interesting. So, why ammonia? Well, because hydrogen itself is a nightmare to ship. It needs extreme compression or cooling to around minus 253 degrees. Which is why I think it\'s never going to'

[01:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'work for transport, particularly for any kind of cars. Ammonia is far denser in energy terms. The world already trades around 20 million tons of it a year,'

[02:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and the ports, ships, and safety standards already exist. So, ammonia becomes the delivery truck for hydrogen, and increasingly a clean shipping fuel in its own right. On the Electric Viking'

[02:19] [scene:talk]
